// This makes `vision/` a project, so `@/packages/bytes/src/buf.wac` in a file under here resolves
// to `vision/packages/bytes/src/buf.wac` rather than being a compile error.
//
// `@/` is the root of the project containing the *importing file* — the nearest directory at or
// above it holding a `wac.json5` — so a file in `vision/` never reaches out of `vision/`, and the
// code reads as though this directory were the repository. The corresponding real thing is always
// at the same path with `vision/` taken off the front.
//
// Empty is a valid manifest and this one is empty on purpose: its presence is the whole question
// `@/` asks, and nothing reads its contents to answer. The repository root has no manifest, which
// is why nothing outside `vision/` writes `@/` today.
//
// **And sixteen imported names in eleven files disagree with the paragraph above**, measured
// 2026-09-04 — seventeen in twelve when this was written, and the one that went was never an
// overlay import at all: `packages/page/src/counter.wac` named `@/packages/page` for `Node`, a type
// `core/jsx.wac` declares, and the package it named does exist. It was found by widening the import
// check from *does the target file exist* to *does the target export this name*, which also found
// three calls to a `Buf.empty()` that `packages/bytes` does not have.
//
// The remaining sixteen are the real thing. They name files the rewrites chose not to write —
// `./stringify.wac`, `@/packages/crypto/src/sha256.wac` — every one of which exists in
// `packages/` at the same path with `vision/` taken off the front. So the code assumes an *overlay*: resolve here, fall through to the repository if
// absent. That is not what this manifest says and it is not something the resolver can express.
// `../QUESTIONS.md` has the two ways out and why the second is a language question rather than a
// tidy-up.
//
// See `vision/packages/README.md`. Nothing in here compiles.
{}
